    <statement><p>On a piece of paper, draw the graph of a continuous function
<m>y=f(x)</m> that satisfies the
following three conditions.
<me>f'(x) &gt; 0\quad\mbox{for}\quad x \lt  -2,</me>
<me>f'(x) \lt  0\quad\mbox{for}\quad -2 \lt  x \lt  2,</me>
<me>f'(x) &gt; 0\quad\mbox{for}\quad 2 \lt  x</me></p>
<p>Approximate your function by picking a segment from the following for
each of the sections of your graph, first for <m>x \lt  -2</m>, then for
<m>-2 \lt  x \lt  2</m>, and then for <m>2 \lt  x</m>.  (You should, of course,
imagine sliding the pieces vertically up or down to make the function
you create be continuous.)</p>
<p>For <m>x \lt  -2</m>, use segment <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>For <m>-2 \lt  x \lt  2</m>, use segment <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>For <m>2 \lt  x</m>, use segment <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
